     <p class="mg-lead">Collector demand generally centers on original prewar steel-string examples. The late 1920s through 1938 combine light construction, red spruce, hide glue, scalloped bracing, and modern geometry. The years 1939 through 1944 remain exceptionally desirable. Late 1944 through 1946 is a feature-sensitive transition.</p>
     <div class="mg-values"><div><h3>Era Premium</h3><p>Prewar pearl Styles 42 and 45 lead, followed by prewar herringbone Style 28. Wartime examples and pre-1969 Brazilian rosewood remain strong. Clean postwar mahogany guitars can be more accessible.</p></div><div><h3>Originality</h3><p>Original finish, bridge, bridge plate, tuners, pickguard, and unaltered wood are decisive. A professional neck reset or refret can preserve value; a refinish, retop, shaved bridge, or oversized plate usually cannot.</p></div><div><h3>Condition and Sound</h3><p>Cracks, distortion, neck angle, brace work, repair quality, setup, and musical response all matter. A clean year does not outrank an earlier guitar with honest professional maintenance by default.</p></div><div><h3>Responsible Pricing</h3><p>Start with recent sold examples of the same size, style, year band, and condition. Asking prices show expectations, not market value. Adjust for rarity, originality, structure, sound, provenance, and timing.</p></div></div>
